Description: Fix GHSA-q4gf-8mx6-v5v3 (CVE-2026-23869) — bound RSC deserialization work to prevent CPU DoS
 A specially crafted HTTP request to any App Router Server Function endpoint is
 deserialized by Next.js's vendored React Server Components flight decoder
 (next/dist/compiled/react-server-dom-webpack/server.{node,edge}). The decoder's
 model-reviver path (initializeModelChunk -> JSON.parse(chunk.value, _fromJSON)
 -> parseModelString) placed no bound on structural array/object nesting depth,
 on the number of decoded structural nodes, or on server-action bound-argument
 array size, so a small request could drive unbounded CPU work (CWE-770 / CWE-400
 during CWE-502 deserialization of untrusted data), yielding denial of service.
 .
 No discrete upstream fix commit exists; the upstream mitigation ships entangled
 in a React 18->19 vendor bump (Next.js 15.5.15 / 16.2.3). This is a re-implemented
 equivalent guard applied directly to the target's own vendored decoder: decodeReply
 accepts an optional { arraySizeLimit } option recorded on the response; before
 JSON.parse, a string-literal-aware O(n) scanner over the raw model string enforces
 a structural nesting-depth cap and (when a limit is opted into) a cumulative
 structural-node cap, throwing 'Maximum array nesting exceeded'. A conservative
 default depth cap (1000) applies even without an explicit limit. Because
 initializeModelChunk's existing try/catch turns the throw into a rejected chunk,
 the awaiting Server Action handler receives a rejected promise instead of doing
 unbounded work. Fully backward compatible with existing callers that pass no option.
 Applied to the four runtime-loaded bundles (server.node + server.edge, development
 and production.min).
